NAME

shoot - Run the Mangrove interactive shell or a script

SYNOPSIS

    shoot [SCRIPT]
    shoot --help

DESCRIPTION

With no script argument, Shoot provides an interactive command prompt. With a
script path, it runs the script and exits with its result. Shoot resolves
installed commands from `/bin`; commands may also be started by executable
path. `help` lists Shoot's built-in commands. Built-ins do not have separate
manual pages.

Spaces and tabs separate arguments. Single and double quotes group text and
are removed. Quoted and unquoted parts can form one argument, and empty quoted
arguments are preserved. Outside quotes, backslash escapes the following
character; inside quotes, backslashes are literal. An unquoted `//` begins a
comment. `$name` expands a variable except inside single quotes; an unset
variable expands to an empty argument.

Unquoted pathname patterns support `*`, `?`, `[abc]`, `[a-z]`, and `[!abc]`
one path component at a time. Results are sorted. Hidden entries match only
when the component pattern begins with a dot. Unmatched patterns remain
literal. Quoting or escaping pattern characters keeps them literal. Shoot
does not perform field splitting, command substitution, or recursive `**`
matching.

Scripts use one command or assignment per line. Variables can be assigned a
value or incremented/decremented by a bounded integer operation. `if`, optional
`else`, `while`, and `end` provide conditional and loop blocks. Conditions
support file existence, string equality, and signed integer comparisons.
Blocks are checked before execution. A later command failure can follow
earlier completed commands; the script then stops with a failure status.

One stdout redirection may be attached to a command:

    command arguments > PATH
    command arguments >> PATH

`>` creates or truncates a regular file. `>>` creates a missing file or
appends. There is no input redirection, stderr redirection, pipeline,
background job, command chaining, or subshell. A command's arguments remain
separate argv entries. Argument preparation must complete before command
execution; filesystem batches can still partially succeed after execution
begins.

OPTIONS

    -h, --help — Show concise invocation help and exit.

KEYBINDINGS

    Left — Move one UTF-8 scalar left.
    Right — Move one UTF-8 scalar right.
    Up — Browse older history entries.
    Down — Browse newer history entries and restore the unfinished draft.
    Alt+Left — Move to the previous word.
    Alt+Right — Move to the next word.
    Home — Move to the beginning of the command.
    End — Move to the end of the command.
    Backspace — Delete the previous scalar.
    Delete — Delete the next scalar.
    Alt+Backspace — Delete to the previous word boundary.
    Alt+Delete — Delete to the next word boundary.
    Tab — Complete a command or built-in at command position; otherwise complete a path.
    Enter — Execute the command.
    Ctrl+C — Cancel current input, or interrupt a foreground command.
    Ctrl+D — Return EOF on an empty line; otherwise delete at the cursor when possible.
    Ctrl+L — Clear the terminal and redraw the prompt and input.
    PgUp — Scroll up one visible page through terminal output.
    PgDn — Scroll down one visible page through terminal output.
    Alt+PgUp — Jump to the oldest retained terminal output.
    Alt+PgDn — Return to live terminal output.

CONFIGURATION

Shoot reads the optional `~/.config/shoot/config` file at startup. `reload`
reads it again. Missing configuration uses these defaults:

    prompt_color=green
    completion_color=dark_gray
    completion_case_sensitive=true
    history_enabled=true
    history_limit=256
    history_persist=true
    history_ignore_duplicates=true
    history_ignore_space=true

`prompt_color` and `completion_color` accept `black`, `red`, `green`,
`yellow`, `blue`, `magenta`, `cyan`, `light_gray`, `dark_gray`,
`bright_red`, `bright_green`, `bright_yellow`, `bright_blue`,
`bright_magenta`, `bright_cyan`, or `white`. The other settings except
`history_limit` accept `true` or `false`. `history_limit` accepts an integer
from 1 through 4096. Lines beginning with `//` are comments. Spaces and tabs
around `=` are accepted; examples use canonical `key=value` form.

Duplicate or unknown keys, malformed assignments, invalid values, and read,
allocation, or close errors reject the entire file. Shoot uses all built-in
defaults rather than applying a valid prefix, prints the path and specific
error (including a line number for parse errors), and continues running. It
does not repair the file. The file is read dynamically with no fixed small
size ceiling; memory availability bounds it.

On upgrade, if the new file is absent but `~/.shoot/config` exists, Shoot reads
the old file and attempts an exclusive copy. The old file is never deleted or
overwritten. If both exist, the new path takes precedence. If a valid old file
cannot be copied, its settings remain usable for the current run and a
warning identifies the migration failure.

Persistent history is stored in `~/.state/shoot/history`, not in the config
file. History is bounded by `history_limit`; writes occur on normal shell exit
and orderly system shutdown or reboot. The parent state directory is created
only when history must be written.

COMMANDS

    jump PATH — Change the current working directory.
    help — List Shoot's built-in commands.
    history — Display or manage command history.
    reload — Reload Shoot's configuration.
    exit — End the current login session.
    logout — End the current login session.

EXAMPLES

    shoot
    shoot build.shoot
    list *.txt
    type notes.txt > notes-copy.txt

NOTES

The interactive command line is bounded, while expanded argv data uses the
native process argument budget and available memory. Script resource limits
are described in `documentation/shell/language.md`. Shoot is a small Mangrove
shell language and does not claim Bash or POSIX shell compatibility.

Terminal scrollback uses the normal screen's 512-row cell ring, including the
visible rows; it is not 512 additional rows. `clear` and Ctrl+L retain the
vacated live screen as the newest history page before homing the prompt. A
clear while viewing history blanks only that presentation. Logind erases
retained terminal contents at session boundaries.

